A route's markup is built at compile time, so anything it renders has to be a
literal or an expression over something known per request.

A page's path parameters are one of those: they arrive as a struct value the
`params` prop names, and the body reaches them the way any body reaches a
scoped slot's arguments -- by populating the slot by name, which is where `v`
is written. Markup over a field of it renders into a hole rather than being
refused, and the dynamic path reaches the Go mux as a pattern rather than a
fixed string.

The path is a plain string -- written backticked here, since `{pkg}` in a
double-quoted one is an interpolation. It used to *be* the interpolation, and
the checker read the placeholders off it and synthesized a var per name. Three
things went with that: a placeholder and a node `#id` shared one namespace with
nothing declaring either, nothing could say a parameter was anything but a
string, and a misspelled placeholder declared a var rather than being reported.
error_window_path_param.sngl is the last of those; the typed case is
route_path_param_typed.txtar.

Two other expression forms had to be taken back from the Go lowering for the
same reason, each in its own archive: a ternary in route_path_param_ternary.txtar
and a list lambda in route_path_param_lambda.txtar.

`main` names root because what it renders is a window, and #214 is what
makes that the only spelling: a component's own body is now asked which family
it renders into, so `component main ui.node` is a check error here rather than a
window a backend swallows without a word.

-- param.sngl --
import "sngl:platform/html"
import nav "sngl:ui/nav"
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        html
    }
}

struct PageParams {
    pkg string
}

ui.window(title="t") {
    nav.stack #pages {
        nav.page #pkg(href=`/p/{pkg}`, title="t", params=PageParams{}) {
            component content(v) {
                html.a(href="/p/" + v.pkg, innerText=v.pkg)
            }
        }
    }
}
-- out/go/html/server.go --
package main

import (
	"net/http"
)

// Handler returns an http.Handler wired with every route.
func Handler() http.Handler {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()
	mux.HandleFunc("GET /p/{pkg}", handlePkg)
	return mux
}

// PageParams is what route "/p/{pkg}" takes from the request.
type PageParams struct {
	Pkg string
}

func handlePkg(__w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {
	__w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "text/html; charset=utf-8")
	__w.Write([]byte("<!-- Code generated by sngl (html) from param.sngl; DO NOT EDIT. -->\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html><head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>t</title>\n  <style>\n* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }\nbody { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }\n  </style>\n</head><body>\n\n<a id=\"$0\">\n</a>\n\n<script>\n(() => {\n  // sngl-entry.js\n  var state = { v: null, visible__inst0: true };\n  state.v = ((__m) => __m ? { ...{}, pkg: decodeURIComponent(__m[1]) } : {})(location.pathname.match(/^\\/p\\/([^/]*)$/));\n  var $0 = document.getElementById(\"$0\");\n  function __sngl_init() {\n    $0.setAttribute(\"href\", \"/p/\" + state.v.pkg);\n    $0.innerText = state.v.pkg;\n  }\n  __sngl_init();\n})();\n</script>\n</body></html>\n"))
}

func main() {
	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", Handler())
}
